WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.160
I'm Benoit Dageville.

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.560
I'm one of the co-founder at Snowflake.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.520
Snowflake is a company whose mission

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:11.160
is to revolutionize access to data and AI in the cloud.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.400
Myself and Thierry Cruanes,

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.440
who is the other co-founder of Snowflake,

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.360
the, the funny thing is that we never wanted to do a company.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.920
Uh, we never wanted to do a startup, really.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.680
We wanted to build a, an amazing product.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.480
We, we spent, you know, literally

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.500
months in my apartment, and we were dreaming.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.640
You have to dream to find the big idea.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.980
You, you, you have not to be scared to dream about what,

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.760
what does it mean if, if, if only that was possible?

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:39.960
What would be the impacts?

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.660
And, and then you work back from that if statement,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.680
and you see maybe we can make it happen.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.560
The number one quality of an amazing product is simplicity.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.880
If you can use a product without knowing a lot about this product,

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:55.760
this product is going to be amazing.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.680
It's always tempting to say, "Oh, I'm going to appeal to everyone."

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.440
When you try to make everyone happy,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.440
no one will be super happy, okay?

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:06.460
Uh, amazing technology, I, I always say,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.260
is built from, you know, arguments and discussion,

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.520
and I disagree with you.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:12.800
I think we should do this.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:13.820
No, we should do that.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.160
We have a saying in, at Snowflake, "Go direct."

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.520
You know, we are human beings,

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.300
so we have conflicts, we have disagreements.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:21.900
So, so it's important to be

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.660
able to do that in a very secure environment where,

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.820
where people feel that they can, they, they can speak up.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:30.100
They, they can say, "No, I,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.900
I think this is, this is not going to work."

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.440
And it doesn't matter, you know,

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.900
who you are, you know, to say these type of things.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.880
To encourage creativity, you,

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.260
you have to encourage, you know, everyone to speak.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.920
And as of today, we are about, you know, 9,000 employees.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.820
The, the number one lesson that we learn, you know,

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.340
to grow a company is to hire someone that you trust.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.600
Otherwise, you, you become the bottleneck.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.760
If you, if you hire someone,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.980
you have to absolutely trust them from day one.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.020
You don't build that trust.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.280
I'm, I'm from Europe.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.100
In Europe, you, you don't trust people.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.000
You start, you know, almost with zero.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.840
You know, as you know these people,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.820
you give them points, so to speak,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.300
and then you fully trust them.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:11.460
You...

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:12.820
In the US is the reverse.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.440
You start with 100%, right?

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.580
You never met this person, you, you, you give full trust,

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.100
and then you remove if you see that it doesn't work.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.220
So you, you have to trust and give,

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.220
you know, someone else the opportunity, you know,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.620
do their thing and, and, you know, this is why you hire them.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.100
So this trust is, is a super critical aspect,

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.600
and, and my take is give it 100% to them from day one.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.580
Don't do the Eu- European thing.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.360
Back when I was building my YC-backed company,

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.100
Polymail, our users kept asking for integrations

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.940
with third-party apps like Salesforce, HubSpot, and Slack.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.220
But we quickly learned how

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.380
exhausting it is to build these integrations,

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.280
not to mention the never-ending maintenance that followed.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.780
That's why we started Paragon.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.020
Paragon is an embedded integration platform that engineering

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:08.600
teams at B2B SaaS companies like Copy.ai, AI21,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.800
and Cinch rely on to ship native integrations seven times faster.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.060
With Paragon, engineers only focus on the business logic,

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.040
not the underlying complexities,

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.480
leading to more closed deals, product usage, retention,

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:22.820
and customer upsells.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.100
Scale your product's integration roadmap today.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.460
Learn more at useparagon.com/eo.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.920
As you can see from my thick French accent,

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.860
I am French, uh, by origin,

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.740
but I lived in the Bay Area, uh, for many, many years.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.260
I came here in '96.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.060
So I did my PhD in database system,

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.820
but I felt that in France there

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:57.540
was no real opportunity to create, you know, new system.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:58.980
And I'm a creator.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:00.780
I'm, I'm, I'm somewhere an artist.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.880
It's stupid to say, but software engineers sometime,

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.460
you know, feel of themself a little bit as artist

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:07.920
because they build new things,

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:11.300
and France was not a good, uh, good environment to do that.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.540
Uh, I did research in France, uh,

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.700
but then I left to join, you know, Oracle, the core development.

00:04:16.899 --> 00:04:18.800
Myself and Thierry Cruanes,

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:20.800
who is the other co-founder of Snowflake,

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:24.920
we were both working in the core database division at Oracle.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:26.840
And you know, the, the, the, the funny

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.720
thing is that we never wanted to do a company.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.300
Uh, we never wanted to do a startup, really.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.800
We wanted to build a, an amazing product.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:38.820
We thought the cloud could, could be a revolution for data access.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.800
Traditionally, people would put data in their data centers,

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.160
and they would, you know, use,

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.640
you know, systems to access this data.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:51.040
We thought with the cloud we could create a solution so simple, uh,

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:53.020
where people would not have to, you know,

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:54.780
manage, you know, infrastructure.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.060
They could, you know, just ask, you know, question to their data,

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.120
and, you know, Snowflake would give them answer.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.120
And, and to do that, we had to do a startup.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.220
We had to do a company.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:08.260
So Thierry and I, we left from, from, um, from Oracle.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.800
And when we started with, with Thierry,

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.680
we had no idea what we were going to do,

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:18.840
and actually we had no idea if we were going to find a great idea.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.620
In, in, in terms of, um, of finding a great idea, it takes time.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:28.180
Uh, we started in August 2012, and we worked until,

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.500
you know, end of 2012 to start to build a team.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:34.020
So I had a small apartment on El Camino.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:37.060
Thierry was coming every day, uh, to my apartment.

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:41.900
We bought a whiteboard, um, we had two laptops, and we kind of...

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.200
We thought completely from the ground up of what,

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.060
what cloud means for these data systems,

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:51.620
how cloud can be impactful, how can we build Snowflake in this

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.680
completely different environment than traditional data centers?

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:56.460
And, and we were dreaming.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.620
You have to dream to find the big idea.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:01.920
You, you, you have not to be scared to dream about what,

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.464
what does it mean if, if, if only That was possible, right?

00:06:06.544 --> 00:06:08.704
What, what is, was, what would be the impacts?

00:06:08.944 --> 00:06:12.424
And, and then you work back from that if statement

00:06:12.464 --> 00:06:14.644
and you see maybe we can make it happen, right?

00:06:14.704 --> 00:06:16.924
It was a big if, it was a little bit of a dream.

00:06:17.304 --> 00:06:19.124
But maybe it's not, and let's

00:06:19.184 --> 00:06:21.604
look at the, the, the, the new technology.

00:06:21.644 --> 00:06:23.324
And that's the other important thing,

00:06:24.084 --> 00:06:27.564
is technology unlocks, you know, new possibilities.

00:06:27.684 --> 00:06:31.764
And I think it's important to understand what new technology

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:36.084
can do to an industry and, and think back a little bit and say,

00:06:36.144 --> 00:06:38.184
"Wow, you know, cloud, you know.

00:06:38.224 --> 00:06:39.404
That's what cloud can do."

00:06:39.724 --> 00:06:41.084
And, and that's really what's...

00:06:41.604 --> 00:06:44.004
What, what we were looking for is how

00:06:44.144 --> 00:06:48.124
cloud can have a dramatic impact on, on this industry,

00:06:48.224 --> 00:06:51.944
which was, you know, super old and, and super boring somewhere.

00:06:52.004 --> 00:06:53.964
The, the world of data, databases,

00:06:54.144 --> 00:06:55.684
uh, I mean, it's, it's, like, boring.

00:06:56.024 --> 00:06:58.444
And what was this great idea was about,

00:06:58.684 --> 00:07:00.804
you know, in the cloud, you, you c- you can, you know,

00:07:01.004 --> 00:07:04.284
get, provision a lot of resources, server, machines.

00:07:04.804 --> 00:07:08.444
So if you have, let's say, 10 machine or if you have 100 machines,

00:07:08.844 --> 00:07:11.884
with 10 times more machines, basically you can go 10 times faster.

00:07:12.444 --> 00:07:14.244
So we say, "Wow," and we know how to do that.

00:07:14.284 --> 00:07:15.784
So we said, "Oh, wow, in the cloud

00:07:16.304 --> 00:07:19.564
we can allocate maybe 100 servers, 1,000 servers,

00:07:19.984 --> 00:07:22.044
and we can go therefore 100 times faster,

00:07:22.264 --> 00:07:24.084
1,000, 1,000 times faster."

00:07:24.744 --> 00:07:25.964
Actually, because we rent

00:07:26.644 --> 00:07:28.884
the computer resources to the cloud provider,

00:07:29.044 --> 00:07:30.684
when we are done, we can return that.

00:07:31.004 --> 00:07:35.204
And so in the cloud, if you go 10 times, 100 times faster,

00:07:35.624 --> 00:07:39.484
you pay exactly the same price because you use these resources,

00:07:39.564 --> 00:07:43.024
you know, 10 times, 100 times, 1,000 times less.

00:07:43.284 --> 00:07:44.564
Even though you have more resources,

00:07:44.644 --> 00:07:47.664
because you use th- them less, you pay the same price.

00:07:47.724 --> 00:07:50.504
So we said, "Fast in the cloud is free."

00:07:50.944 --> 00:07:52.844
And if we can do that, and th-

00:07:52.924 --> 00:07:55.304
this was of course technically very challenging,

00:07:55.724 --> 00:07:58.044
but if we can do that, we can, you know,

00:07:58.124 --> 00:08:00.644
basically build a system that can go 10 times,

00:08:00.804 --> 00:08:04.164
100 times faster than any system that exists today,

00:08:04.584 --> 00:08:07.244
I mean, at the time, and yet it will not cost,

00:08:07.324 --> 00:08:08.884
you know, for our customers more.

00:08:08.944 --> 00:08:10.324
So that's the killer idea.

00:08:10.764 --> 00:08:12.364
And, and when we had this idea,

00:08:12.864 --> 00:08:14.763
we, we said, "If we can make it happen,

00:08:14.983 --> 00:08:18.424
okay, if we can build it, then we have an amazing company."

00:08:18.584 --> 00:08:20.164
So, so we knew that this is...

00:08:20.224 --> 00:08:24.104
was going to be a revolution, and actually it became a revolution.

00:08:24.164 --> 00:08:26.564
We started to have one customer, two customers,

00:08:26.984 --> 00:08:28.844
and they were blown away by, by what

00:08:28.884 --> 00:08:31.784
you could do with Snowflake and, and without complexity.

00:08:31.824 --> 00:08:33.104
It was simple to use.

00:08:33.464 --> 00:08:35.824
Because we remove, you know, all the friction,

00:08:36.344 --> 00:08:39.704
we were able to, to get more and more customers and, you know,

00:08:39.784 --> 00:08:41.144
people would talk to each other.

00:08:41.504 --> 00:08:43.344
And then it, it gets to this viral,

00:08:43.984 --> 00:08:47.144
you know, state where, you know, it, it, it, it exploded.

00:08:47.524 --> 00:08:49.884
We were very worried about that because we said,

00:08:50.424 --> 00:08:52.844
"If we show that Snowflake is so different,

00:08:53.484 --> 00:08:55.464
people are, are going to be worried, too."

00:08:55.524 --> 00:08:56.684
At the same time, if you say,

00:08:57.204 --> 00:08:59.964
"It's ex- exactly the same as what you are using," you know,

00:09:00.044 --> 00:09:01.344
people are not going to adopt you.

00:09:01.404 --> 00:09:03.044
So you have to find a good balance.

00:09:03.184 --> 00:09:05.704
Sometime you have to explain

00:09:05.784 --> 00:09:08.864
yourself in comparison to existing technology.

00:09:09.084 --> 00:09:11.264
We, for example, use same,

00:09:11.324 --> 00:09:14.664
you know, terminology that was used in the old days.

00:09:14.724 --> 00:09:17.204
So we had, you know, system called warehouse.

00:09:17.264 --> 00:09:18.564
You know, everyone knew warehouse.

00:09:18.624 --> 00:09:20.244
That's a familiar concept.

00:09:20.304 --> 00:09:21.584
So you have to use, you know,

00:09:22.124 --> 00:09:25.984
existing conte- concepts, uh, such that you can help,

00:09:26.084 --> 00:09:29.904
you know, people go your way and, and, and your revolution.

00:09:30.264 --> 00:09:32.384
So somewhere, if you say it's a revolution,

00:09:32.484 --> 00:09:34.664
it's, it's possible that it will not work.

00:09:34.884 --> 00:09:38.764
S- so you have to bring them slowly, uh, not too fast.

00:09:39.124 --> 00:09:39.684
So it's tricky.

00:09:39.884 --> 00:09:40.684
It's, uh, very tricky.

00:09:40.704 --> 00:09:43.504
It's a lot of, uh, explanation how you explain it,

00:09:43.624 --> 00:09:45.444
how you make it look, you know,

00:09:45.524 --> 00:09:48.584
familiar somewhere, uh, even though it's very different.

00:09:49.284 --> 00:09:50.584
So, so it's an art.

00:09:55.964 --> 00:09:59.684
The number one quality of an amazing product is simplicity.

00:10:00.324 --> 00:10:04.504
If you can use a product without knowing a lot about this product,

00:10:05.004 --> 00:10:06.924
this product is going to be amazing, right?

00:10:07.104 --> 00:10:11.204
Th- think of cars, thinks of iPhone, thinks of, of simple product.

00:10:11.244 --> 00:10:12.864
Th- these are the one where you say, "Ha ha.

00:10:13.124 --> 00:10:13.464
Wow."

00:10:13.944 --> 00:10:18.104
And a product which is simple but at the same time so powerful.

00:10:18.824 --> 00:10:21.704
People who have complex products but expose this

00:10:21.764 --> 00:10:24.843
complexity to the end users are not making great products.

00:10:25.324 --> 00:10:29.664
So for me, it's, first of all, you know, simplicity, but,

00:10:29.744 --> 00:10:30.964
but underneath, you know,

00:10:31.184 --> 00:10:33.424
uh, again, if you take the analogy of the iPhone,

00:10:33.484 --> 00:10:35.344
the iPhone is nothing but simple,

00:10:35.924 --> 00:10:37.684
uh, tech- piece of technology, right?

00:10:37.744 --> 00:10:39.844
It's probably the most advanced piece of technology.

00:10:39.984 --> 00:10:41.264
But when you use, you know,

00:10:41.344 --> 00:10:43.784
this product, you know, they are, you know, simple.

00:10:43.844 --> 00:10:47.944
So that's, to me, i- is probably the number one, uh,

00:10:48.104 --> 00:10:49.464
aspect of a great product.

00:10:49.784 --> 00:10:52.324
You know, take three fingers and, you know,

00:10:52.404 --> 00:10:55.764
list all the things that you could do potentially, but,

00:10:55.824 --> 00:10:57.684
but try to limit it to few,

00:10:57.804 --> 00:11:00.424
very few important thing, and, and you rank.

00:11:00.484 --> 00:11:02.124
You, you just say, y- you know,

00:11:02.244 --> 00:11:04.464
"Our product obviously need this and that."

00:11:04.804 --> 00:11:06.104
It's always tempting to say,

00:11:06.144 --> 00:11:09.704
"Oh, I'm going to appeal to everyone," uh, uh,

00:11:09.824 --> 00:11:11.164
and then you do a bad job, right?

00:11:11.244 --> 00:11:13.844
Because you, you know, you, you try to make everyone happy.

00:11:14.044 --> 00:11:15.544
When you try to make everyone happy,

00:11:16.284 --> 00:11:18.044
no one will be super happy, okay?

00:11:18.124 --> 00:11:18.924
I, I can tell you.

00:11:19.384 --> 00:11:21.884
Uh, uh, so the way to streamline,

00:11:21.944 --> 00:11:25.324
you know, is both to, to, to really focus on few things

00:11:25.724 --> 00:11:29.884
and also to make these few things focus on, you know, few persona.

00:11:30.044 --> 00:11:32.244
Yeah, don't, don't try to appeal to everyone.

00:11:32.844 --> 00:11:34.284
We, we, we have a saying in...

00:11:34.324 --> 00:11:35.684
at Snowflake, "Go direct."

00:11:35.844 --> 00:11:39.444
We are human beings, so we have conflicts, we have disagreements,

00:11:40.064 --> 00:11:42.604
y- and, and that's very important to recognize, right?

00:11:43.404 --> 00:11:45.844
A- amazing technology, I, I always say,

00:11:46.024 --> 00:11:49.644
is built from, you know, arguments and discussion,

00:11:49.724 --> 00:11:50.924
and I disagree with you.

00:11:51.004 --> 00:11:52.184
I think we should do this.

00:11:52.244 --> 00:11:53.204
No, we should do that.

00:11:53.664 --> 00:11:56.224
And, and these discussions are the thing

00:11:56.284 --> 00:11:59.264
that, that really makes the best product at the end, right?

00:11:59.704 --> 00:12:01.984
Because you, you get to this consensus.

00:12:02.044 --> 00:12:03.944
You understand, you know, different opinions.

00:12:04.304 --> 00:12:05.584
So, so it's important to be

00:12:05.624 --> 00:12:09.344
able to do that in a very secure environment where,

00:12:09.404 --> 00:12:12.484
where people feel that they can They, they can speak up.

00:12:12.624 --> 00:12:13.784
They, they can say, "No, I,

00:12:13.924 --> 00:12:15.584
I think this is, this is not going to work."

00:12:15.784 --> 00:12:17.624
And, and it doesn't matter, you know,

00:12:17.744 --> 00:12:20.064
who you are, you know, to say these type of things.

00:12:20.124 --> 00:12:22.504
So, so, so I think you have to encourage,

00:12:22.564 --> 00:12:24.304
you know, to encourage creativity,

00:12:24.724 --> 00:12:27.204
you, you have to encourage, you know, everyone to speak.

00:12:31.564 --> 00:12:33.204
The, the most important to grow

00:12:33.304 --> 00:12:36.764
is, is, is indeed to hire someone that you trust.

00:12:36.824 --> 00:12:39.004
Otherwise, you, you become the bottleneck,

00:12:39.164 --> 00:12:41.644
and, and you don't want to, to have th- this,

00:12:41.904 --> 00:12:43.784
this full control of, of everything.

00:12:44.304 --> 00:12:48.604
So how you gain, get, you gain this trust is interesting, right?

00:12:48.684 --> 00:12:52.264
I, I remember actually the first hire, uh, we had at Snowflake,

00:12:52.324 --> 00:12:55.204
our first one, was asking a lot of question.

00:12:55.284 --> 00:12:58.604
Before he wanted to sign, he was asking tons of question,

00:12:58.704 --> 00:13:00.564
you know, like, "Okay, how is it going to work?"

00:13:00.624 --> 00:13:01.484
Like, suspicious.

00:13:01.964 --> 00:13:02.844
And he said, "Benoit..."

00:13:02.924 --> 00:13:04.064
He told me one day, "You know,

00:13:04.124 --> 00:13:06.144
Benoit, I'm asking all these question."

00:13:06.184 --> 00:13:07.144
He was from Europe.

00:13:07.184 --> 00:13:08.984
He say, "You know, I'm, I'm from Europe.

00:13:09.384 --> 00:13:11.724
In Europe, you, you don't trust people.

00:13:11.864 --> 00:13:13.604
You start, you know, almost with zero.

00:13:14.264 --> 00:13:16.424
And then, you know, as you know these people,

00:13:16.544 --> 00:13:17.924
as you start to trust them,

00:13:18.444 --> 00:13:21.544
you, you, you know, you give them points, so to speak,

00:13:21.624 --> 00:13:23.044
and then you fully trust them.

00:13:23.084 --> 00:13:23.324
You...

00:13:23.364 --> 00:13:26.864
But you start at zero, and, and you build this trust over time."

00:13:27.384 --> 00:13:28.964
He said, "In the US, it's the reverse.

00:13:29.064 --> 00:13:30.564
You start with 100%", right?

00:13:30.664 --> 00:13:33.824
You never met this person, you, you, you give full trust,

00:13:33.864 --> 00:13:36.344
and then you remove if you see that it doesn't work.

00:13:36.884 --> 00:13:39.004
And, and I would say the, the, the biggest,

00:13:40.124 --> 00:13:43.684
you know, thing for me is that from day one, you give full trust.

00:13:44.164 --> 00:13:46.004
If you, if you hire someone,

00:13:46.424 --> 00:13:49.224
you have to absolutely trust them from day one.

00:13:49.264 --> 00:13:50.264
You don't build that trust.

00:13:50.564 --> 00:13:51.644
It could not work.

00:13:51.744 --> 00:13:54.044
We made some, you know, hiring mistake,

00:13:54.104 --> 00:13:57.284
and we discover that, oh, this is not working, you know.

00:13:57.344 --> 00:14:00.084
If, if it's w- w- for one reason or another,

00:14:00.504 --> 00:14:01.824
then you can remove this trust.

00:14:01.884 --> 00:14:04.164
But you, you start 100%.

00:14:04.224 --> 00:14:06.144
You, you have to trust and give, you know,

00:14:06.784 --> 00:14:10.524
uh, uh, give someone else the opportunity y- you know,

00:14:10.644 --> 00:14:13.984
do their thing and, and, you know, this is why you hire them.

00:14:14.044 --> 00:14:17.344
So this trust is, is a super critical aspect,

00:14:17.424 --> 00:14:20.764
and, and my take is give it 100% to them from day one.

00:14:21.004 --> 00:14:22.744
Don't do the Eu- European thing.

